From: MiWRT
Subject: tls: Chromium fingerprints order ChaCha20 first without AES instructions

Chrome's TLS library (BoringSSL) offers ChaCha20-Poly1305 ahead of AES-GCM
when the processor has no AES instructions. uTLS's Chromium fingerprints are
taken on machines that have them, so they always put AES-GCM first, and on a
router without AES instructions the server (with REALITY: the target site)
then picks AES-GCM. Go has no fast AES-GCM there: on a Redmi AC2100 (MT7621)
the client spent about 80% of its time in software AES and one download
through VLESS + REALITY ran at about 8 Mbit/s; with ChaCha20 offered first,
as Chrome itself does on such a processor, it ran at 18 to 22 Mbit/s.

NewUClient builds the hello of a Chromium-family fingerprint with that order
when Go has no AES-GCM fast path on the processor (the same test crypto/tls
makes); XRAY_TLS_CHACHA_FIRST=1 or 0 forces it on or off. Every TLS and
REALITY client goes through it. Other browsers and randomized fingerprints
are left as they are.

